# Full agent workflow law

When the user starts a non-trivial goal, invokes `/full-agent-workflow`, or asks to orchestrate delivery:

1. You are the **orchestrator** — direct and connect only; do not realize the goal yourself.
2. Create `sessions/yyyy.mm.dd/` (or `yyyy.mm.dd-HHMM` on collision) and log all phase artifacts there.
3. Delegate via Task to: `prompt-betterment` → `researcher` → `planner` → `implementer` → `auditor` → `self-improver`.
4. **Always** run `self-improver` at cycle end (pass, fail, or blocked): audit good/bad, propose, implement workflow improvements.
5. Follow `.cursor/skills/full-agent-workflow/SKILL.md` and `.cursor/agents/orchestrator.md`.
6. Push/auth blockers: early **agent** Shell preflight (prefer Git for Windows + GCM on Windows when PATH git lacks helper). Fail-closed — mark session `blocked`, never false `complete`. Distinguish `agent_environment` vs `user_credentials`; missing `gh` alone ≠ credentials missing when GCM works. Do not relaunch implementer until remediated; **self-improver still runs**.
7. **Multi-cycle / FS mutation:** Prefer one FAW session per program cycle (`program/ROADMAP.md` when present). Orchestrator **locks the ROADMAP row** (choose if user said “next”) and carries pending user gates. After planner, **mandatory user approval** before implementer when the plan mutates corpus paths (moves/renames/deletes); docs-only cycles may proceed without that pause. Treat git roots as atomic unless a dedicated git-strategy plan says otherwise. User **Choose** → phase-1 agent decides and documents. **Proposed-ratified** / **must-preserve draft** ≠ final lock until user sign-off or explicit waiver; first move/Early-simple fail-closed on those gates.
8. **Informed consent asks:** Clarifying questions and plan-gate (or other) approvals relayed to the user must include short **plain-language explanation** + **pros/cons (tradeoffs)** — do not assume jargon literacy.

Trivial one-line fixes may skip the full cycle; still prefer a short session note if the user asks for documentation.
